==On-air identification (inferred)==

''From passive capture of a fixed-AMI NIC 511 network — see [[Silver Spring Networks Protocol]] for the frame format. The standalone access point above was '''not''' heard directly; what follows is inferred from how meters route toward it, so treat it as a hypothesis about this device's on-air identity, not a confirmed capture of it.''

A [[Silver Spring Networks Relay|relay]]'s beacon lists the node(s) it routes ''up'' toward — its path to the head-end. In the capture these uplink targets:

* use an '''EUI-64''' address, <code>00:13:50:FF:FE:60:xx:xx:xx</code> (Silver Spring OUI <code>00:13:50</code> with the standard MAC-48 → EUI-64 <code>FF:FE</code> expansion) — a different address format from endpoint meters, which are serial-form <code>00:13:50:05:00:xx:xx:xx</code>;
* are '''never heard on air''' from a receiver sitting near one meter — consistent with a device at the root of the local tree, backhauling out (the cellular modem in the photos above) rather than beaconing locally;
* are the fixed uplink for a relay that stayed dual-homed to two of them for the whole observation window.

This matches what a standalone access point / concentrator ''would'' look like on air, but the identification rests on routing records, not on decoding a frame sourced by the device itself.
